# Workspace guidance (template)

Copy this to `CLAUDE.md` in your workspace or repo root (or merge it into what
`/init` generated). It is the short version of the loop; the full playbook with
scenarios and prompts is **[DEVELOPER_GUIDE.html](DEVELOPER_GUIDE.html)**.

## The default loop for any change with logic

1. **Write it clean** — standards apply while writing, not after. Before writing
   or changing code, load the `clean-code-martin` and `code-as-prose` skills and
   write to them:
   - Reads like well-written prose (Booch): top to bottom, one idea per line,
     clarity over cleverness. If it needs a comment to be understood, rewrite it.
   - Names reveal intent and tell the truth about behaviour; no abbreviations
     (`user` not `usr`). When a rule changes, rename everything that described it.
   - Functions do one thing, ≤ ~20 lines, ≤ 3 parameters (value object beyond that).
   - One responsibility per class; depend on interfaces; inject dependencies.
   - Validate at the edge, fail fast, specific exceptions with context.
   - No magic numbers, no duplication, comments explain *why* only. Constants only
     when they add meaning — never `X = Y + 1` or a restated obvious value.
   - Match the surrounding code's idiom. Boy-scout rule: leave touched code cleaner.
   Ask `@code-guardian` where logic should live *before* writing when the
   structure isn't obvious.
2. **Test it first (TDD)** — RED → GREEN → COMMIT, one behaviour per commit.
   Run one focused test in the inner loop and the full suite once at the end
   (or let CI). A failing test is never deleted or weakened to ship.
3. **Review it** — before any PR: `@code-guardian` (standards) and `/code-review`
   (correctness) on the branch vs main; `/security-review` when it touches auth,
   input, secrets or external calls. Fix **every** verified finding, style
   included, then re-review. Nothing is deferred as "future work".

## Match effort to the task (route by knowledge gap)

| Task | Route |
|---|---|
| Config / infra / dep bump | No nWave, no new test. Do it, run the suite; Guardian only if a mistake could break production (networking, secrets, changed client API) |
| Bug | `/nw-bugfix` (cause known) or `/nw-root-why` first; the reproduction test stays as the regression net |
| Feature / endpoint | `/nw-discuss → /nw-design → /nw-distill → /nw-deliver`, lean rigor, ships with tests |
| Refactor | `/nw-hotspot → /nw-refactor`; green through every step |
| Big module (semi-greenfield) | add `/nw-devops`, `standard` rigor |
| New service (greenfield) | all 7 waves from `/nw-discover` |

Every feature ends `DISTILL → DELIVER`. Start at the earliest wave with real
uncertainty; skip the rest. Default rigor is **lean** (`/nw-rigor lean`).

## Repo rules win

Read the repo's `AGENTS.md` / `CLAUDE.md` before designing; committed guidance
beats this file. Add your stack's facts here (architecture, build and test
commands, which test files are unit vs integration).

## Tools

- `@code-guardian` — 12 standards (Clean Code, Code-as-Prose, SOLID, defensive
  programming, concurrency, error handling, testing pyramid, API design,
  database patterns, observability, code review, refactoring).
- `/code-review`, `/simplify`, `/security-review` — Claude Code's built-in reviewers.
- nWave `/nw-*` — cheat sheet in DEVELOPER_GUIDE.html.
